{
  "format": 1,
  "about": "End-to-end cases: each case observes some raw requests, learns a caveat set from them, seals it into a token, then checks more raw requests against that token. Every case is run twice, once against a v1 token (which names no run) and once against a v2 token sealed for a run: a run id may change no answer anywhere in this file, so one expectation covers both. No caveat or valid token is ever written here: the only way to a valid token is observe, learn, seal. 'bad_tokens' are hand-written malformed tokens, each checked against the request 'GET https api.example.com /orders'; none may ever be allowed. (A hand-written token that is exactly canonical is allowed today, because tokens are not signed yet; that known gap is pinned by a unit test in gate, never by a vector.) Expected values are 'allowed' or the snake_case of a gate::Denial variant. A request may also carry an optional 'mcp' object with 'method' and 'name', the two mirrored MCP header values; a request that carries it has an mcp target and one that does not has an http target, and the two never stand in for each other.",
  "cases": [
